          ":question/problem": "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince $det(A)=0,$ it must be $Ax=0$ has non-trivial solution, which means $\\text{Null}(A)$ has basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}.$*$\\,\u2713$\n\nIndeed, $A$ is not invertible because $det(A)=0.$  Since $A$ is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $det(A)=0,$ then $Ax=0$ has non-trivial solution, which means that $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""
