# Sentence completion using the invertible matrix theorem

The learner first reads a hypothesis about a real square matrix, infers whether its rank is full, evaluates three conclusions using the invertible matrix theorem, and selects the true conclusions. There is no matrix arithmetic. The introduction in the supplied lesson, “The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity,” derives trivial null space, zero nullity, all columns pivot, rank n, and full column space from invertibility. Its final equivalence supports reversing these implications. The canonical worked example e-9961 (c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a) supplies trivial null space and tests determinant, rank and pivots. The adjacent prerequisite example e-5424 supplies a nontrivial null space; no prerequisite records are supplied separately.

The current source has 16 questions. Fifteen are complete moderate items. Six use three text-labelled Boolean checkbox fields; nine use a five-choice radio field selecting a Roman-numeral subset. Replay must retain these complete banks, field shapes, conclusions and explanations. For example q-47599 (7f4e1182-d195-5cca-bb1a-601ea1f9c921) tests determinant, column space, and pivot count; q-142475 (795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a) tests invertibility, rank and column space; q-141205 (a20e0f6e-9a4b-59f0-b28f-3a10b5209120) has three true conclusions, so constant correct-choice count is not a skill constraint. Source q-141342 has a correct nonzero-nullity premise and key but quotes zero nullity in three worked conditionals: replay corrects those three quotations, retaining the original evidence. q-47586 lacks a key, solution and difficulty and its correct subset III only is missing from the bank. It is retained as incomplete evidence, not sampled.

Moderate is the default and only supported band. Easy and hard lack supporting evidence for different workloads. Source labels are retained. Three conclusions, one hypothesis, real square matrices of positive dimension, theorem recall followed by three individual implications and a final subset are fixed to preserve this workload. Each source profile keeps its conclusion concepts, numeric dimension and response shape. Fixed dimensions are contextual, not a claimed source of meaningful variation. No plots are used; the lesson's theorem is typeset math.

The previous implementation and its checks remain in the workspace as historical evidence. It offers 18 semantic records but all nine profiles have one fixed answer vector. The exact relocation failure and this exhaustive shortcut probe are saved in evidence/initial-failure.json and evidence/initial-shortcut.json. A self-contained local copy of the shared nonempty-checkbox policy fixes the undeclared scripts import. The current source adds five complete profiles absent from the old snapshot; these must also replay.

Conservative extensions keep each profile's three conclusion concepts, but permit their positive and negative theorem forms. For example, full rank versus deficient rank, zero versus nonzero determinant, trivial versus nontrivial null space, full versus proper column space, and all versus fewer than n pivot columns. This is supported by the positive and negative source cases q-47587, q-139978, q-141217 and q-47604. A no-basis assertion can become a has-basis assertion: every finite-dimensional null space has a basis, including the empty basis for the zero space. This mathematical invariant is explained rather than treated as an incidental cue. Source wrong ambient dimensions and non-full exact pivot counts remain available on replay; normal variants use full/proper-space and full/deficient-pivot alternatives to avoid asking for an undetermined singular rank.

Choose an eligible correct subset first, then a full-rank or singular hypothesis, then the conclusion forms that realize that subset. Radio profiles use exactly their five source subset choices; checkbox profiles permit each of the seven nonempty subsets. Both singular and invertible cases are eligible when the hypothesis is not simply one of the conclusions. Select a hypothesis concept not among the conclusion concepts; this avoids copying a given assertion. The theorem supports the alternate hypothesis without another reasoning method. Sample independent presentation order of radio choices only after this construction; this is not counted as semantic variety. No new distractor banks or extra response options are introduced.

Every source profile must exercise every eligible correct subset, both matrix statuses, and each eligible hypothesis concept. Controlled changes hold dimension, profile, hypothesis concept and conclusion concepts fixed while changing (a) the matrix status or (b) a conclusion's polarity; the visible key, solution and native grading must change together. Changing to an equivalent hypothesis must preserve the conclusions and key. Report semantic records by visible hypothesis and propositions, not seed or UUID. Check label, position, subset count and profile cues before and after the actual delivery filter. Source replay is a fidelity path; normal sampling uses the decision-variable construction. No claim about live learner-specific selection is possible without learner state; inspect its static filtering rule read-only and report that limit.

Inventory assessment distinguishes stored originals from generated records. Original source questions must never acquire generated provenance. Only a reproduced stored generated record with matching metadata can be listed in affected-inventory.json. A fixed response in a single original is not evidence that the original itself is mathematically wrong; repeated-template weakness is assessed separately from item correctness.

Visual validation found that the source's horizontal theorem equivalence clipped in the actual Learning UI, also affecting the first generated draft. The runtime now stacks invertibility, the equivalence arrow, and the four aligned identities vertically. Source replay compares all four mathematical identities and the exact normalized remaining implication paragraphs, rather than demanding the clipped arrangement. Five final UI screenshots were inspected after this repair.

Final evidence separates the claims: 15 complete source banks replay (81 choices, including 54 distractors); 316 decision records pass independent visible-statement rank witnesses; 1,855 native field responses pass, including correct and incorrect choices; all 316 records survive the actual delivery filter. The 14 recognizable concept/dimension/response profiles include the merged identical concept profiles q-142475 and q-79334. Within 106 identical printed-hypothesis groups, correct subsets, correct counts and correct positions change. The requested five-item sample also survives delivery. This tests declared decision roles and specific incidental cues, not every possible strategy. Learner-specific history and live policy selection remain untested.
